eviations used in this paper:</italic> GC, guanylyl cyclase&#x0003b; IBMX, 3-isobutyl-1-methylxanthine&#x0003b; PDE, phosphodiesterase.</p></fn></fn-group></back><floats-wrap><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Absorbance spectrum of 11-cis 13-demethyl rhodopsin. Transverse absorbance measurements were averaged from four rods after bleach and regeneration with 11-cis 13-demethylretinal (&#x025aa;). A pigment absorbance template (smooth curve) was calculated by nonlinear curve-fitting of an exponential template for a vitamin A1 visual pigment to estimate the peak wavelength (488 nm), maximum pigment absorbance (0.11), and residual absorbance (0.02) at long wavelengths (<xref ref-type="bib" rid="Stavengaetal1993">Stavenga et al. 1993</xref>). (Inset) The normalized bleaching difference spectrum (&#x02022;) of this photolabile pigment in a single cell in the presence of hydroxylamine.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8221.f1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Effect of 11-cis 13-demethylretinal on the membrane dark current and on the stimulus&#x02013;response relations in a rod. Shown is a series of flash responses from one cell in its dark-adapted state (A), after a 5% bleach (B), and in 100 &#x003bc;m 11-cis 13-demethylretinal solution (C). The corresponding intensity&#x02013;response curves for the peak amplitude of the change in the dark current as a function of flash intensity are shown in D: dark-adapted (&#x025aa;), bleach adapted (&#x025b4;), and in 11-cis 13-demethylretinal (&#x02022;).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8221.f2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Effect of 11-cis 13-demethylretinal on the kinetics of dim flash responses in a rod. Normalized dim flash responses from one rod in a dark-adapted state, after a 5% bleach, and in the presence of 100 &#x003bc;m 11-cis 13-demethylretinal. The flash intensities were 0.9, 2.7, and 27 photons/&#x003bc;m<sup>2</sup> and the corresponding response amplitudes were 2.9, 6.4, and 4.4 pA, respectively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8221.f3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Effect of 11-cis 13-demethylretinal on the rate of guanylyl cyclase in a rod. (Left) The current recordings from a rod during steps into 0.5 mM IBMX solution. (Right) The derivative of the cube root of the normalized current, <italic>d</italic>( <italic>J</italic> <sup>1/3</sup>)/<italic>dt</italic>. The time course of the solution step for both panels is shown at the top. Recordings were made first in the dark-adapted state (A), then after a 5% bleach (B), immediately after exposing the cell to 100 &#x003bc;m 11-cis 13-demethylretinal solution (C), and 30 min after exposing the cell to 11-cis 13-demethylretinal (D). Each current trace is the average of six measurements. All recordings were done in the same cell.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8221.f4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Time course of the recovery of guanylyl cyclase after its initial acceleration by 11-cis 13-demethylretinal in a rod. Each data point represents one measurement of the rate of cyclase done by exposing the cell to IBMX solution. Shown are the dark-adapted rate of cyclase (Dark), its steady state bleach-adapted rate after 5% bleach (bleach adapted) and its change as a result of two repetitive treatments with 11-cis 13-demethylretinal (13DM, arrows). The recovery from the initial activation of cyclase by the first treatment with 11-cis 13-demethylretinal is fit by a single exponential decay function with a time constant of 22.4 min.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8221.f5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Effect of 11-cis 13-demethylretinal on the rate of cGMP phosphodiesterase in a rod. (Left) The current from a rod during steps into Li<sup>+</sup> solution. (Right) The normalized current on a semilogarithmic plot. The time course of the solution step is shown at the top. Recordings were made first in the dark-adapted state (A), and then after a 5% bleach (B), immediately after exposing the cell to 100 &#x003bc;m 11-cis 13-demethylretinal solution (C), and 30 min after exposing the cell to 11-cis 13-demethylretinal (D). Each current trace is the average of six measurements. All recordings were done in the same cell.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8221.f6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Effect of 11-cis 13-demethylretinal on the dark current and on the sensitivity in a cone. Shown is a series of flash responses from one cell in its dark-adapted state (A), after a 90% bleach (B), and in 100 &#x003bc;m 11-cis 13-demethylretinal solution (C). The corresponding intensity&#x02013;response curves are shown in D: dark-adapted (&#x025aa;), bleach adapted (&#x025b4;), and in 11-cis 13-demethylretinal (&#x02022;).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8221.f7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Effect of 11-cis 13-demethylretinal on the kinetics of dim-flash responses in a cone. Normalized dim-flash responses from one cone in a dark-adapted state, after a 90% bleach, and in the presence of 100 &#x003bc;m 11-cis 13-demethylretinal. The flash intensities were 70, 21,700, and 6,900 photons/&#x003bc;m<sup>2</sup>, and the corresponding response amplitudes were 1.1, 2.6, and 2.9 pA, respectively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8221.f8"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F9"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Effect of 11-cis 13-demethylretinal on the rate of guanylyl cyclase in a cone. (Left) The current recordings from a cone during steps into 0.5 mM IBMX solution. (Right) The derivative of the cube root of the normalized current, <italic>d</italic>( <italic>J</italic> <sup>1/3</sup>)/<italic>dt</italic>. The time course of the solution step for both panels is shown at the top. Recordings were made first in the dark-adapted state (A), then after a 90% bleach (B), and in 100 &#x003bc;m 11-cis 13-demethylretinal solution (C). Each current trace is the average of four measurements. All recordings were done in the same cell.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8221.f9"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F10"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>A model of the mechanism of activation by 11-cis 13-demethylretinal of phototransduction in rods. (A) rhodopsin, (B) photoactivated rhodopsin (Meta II), (C) free opsin, (D) noncovalent 11-cis 13-demethylretinal &#x02022; opsin complex, (E) equilibrium mixture of free opsin, noncovalent 11-cis 13-demethylretinal &#x02022; opsin complexes, and 11-cis 13-demethyl rhodopsin.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JGP8221.f10"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>